nodecode-fxmacrodata is a rewrite of fxmacrodata's fxmacrodata/hermes-fxmacrodata:
https://github.com/fxmacrodata/hermes-fxmacrodata
commit 9d07f0f9ba5303488b11cffd8133b5342f7e05c4
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Robert Tidball (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's two wires and its hundred tools, in Common
Lisp. The REST client (fixed origin, path members substituted, declared query
members, X-API-Key in a header, the JSON body of the one read-only POST), the
MCP client (initialize, the initialized notification, tools/call, the session
header and the event-stream capture), the envelope every tool answers with
(operation, data, an additive records projection, source_url, citations,
provider_url and the free-tier notices), the JSON-aware credential redaction,
the argument pre-flight (currency shape, date shape and order, no credential
in an argument), the two composed tools (the USD macro brief and the
version-pinned indicator-history snapshot), the /fxmacrodata command, the
manual topic and the tool inventory itself, carried as data read from the
plugin's own packaged operation list.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes plugin host (register_tool,
register_skill, register_system_prompt_section and the agent.secret_scope
secret API) is replaced by the cell declaration -- the section, one manual
topic and NLE:REGISTER-TOOL -- so the skill text and the system-prompt
section live in the one manual topic. jsonschema validation of every call is
replaced by the host's own pre-flight for the members a schema marks
required, plus this cell's shape checks. MCP tool discovery (tools/list) and
the bounded SSE stream reader are not carried: the 57 hosted tools are
registered from the plugin's packaged list, and a stream call reads one
bounded capture instead of an open stream. Redirects are not refused, because
the host's one HTTP verb follows them. The plugin's post-call session DELETE
is dropped: every call opens and finishes its own session, and nothing is
kept between calls.
